  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 325 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Not quite as warm today, with isolated late day\nshowers and storms possible. Becoming more unsettled tomorrow,\nwith more widespread showers and storms likely. A few storms\ncould be strong to severe, with damaging wind gusts, large hail,\nand localized flooding as the main threats.\n\nLatest analysis reveals a weak and rather diffuse frontal\nboundary just north of the VA/NC border, extending W/NW into S\nWV/N KY. To the north, 1022+mb sfc high pressure centered over\nthe Eastern Great Lakes region will nudge this front farther\nsouth as it sags into the region later today. Light E-NE flow\nushers in a slightly cooler and drier airmass, with highs today\nin the mid to upper 80s. Would expect most areas to remain dry\ntoday. However, the boundary dropping south of the region could\nagain be the focus for isolated to widely scattered showers and\nstorms, with a better chance for storms south of US-460 in south\ncentral VA into NE NC. A few showers will be possible tonight\nin association with remnants from decaying Ohio Valley MCS\nactivity. Otherwise, warm and muggy with lows in the low to mid\n70s.\n\nOn Sunday, the expansive ridge over the southern tier of the\ncountry begins to break down, as another shortwave trough digs\nacross the Ohio Valley. Return southerly flow brings hot\ntemperatures and tropical moisture right back into the region\nSunday and Monday. PW values climb back up over 2-2.25\" Sunday,\nwith highs warming back into the low to mid 90s (heat indices\nmax out ~100F). Improved forcing for ascent ahead of the trough\nwill likely trigger scattered to numerous afternoon/evening\nshowers and storms. High PW and strong moisture transport will\nsupport water loaded downdrafts capable of producing strong to\nlocally severe wind gusts. SPC has added a Slight Risk for\nSevere Storms for much of the area Sunday, with a Marginal Risk\nover MD counties and for VA south of US-58 into northeast NC. \nAdditionally, slow- moving or back- building storms present \nanother flash flood threat, once again especially for flood \nprone, poor drainage, and urban areas. WPC has maintained a \nSlight Risk Excessive Rainfall Outlook over the MD Eastern \nShore, with a Marginal Risk over all but out far SW tier of \ncounties. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid with afternoon storms Monday \nahead of the next cold front.\n\nThe hot, humid conditions look to peak on Monday ahead of an\napproaching cold front. Highs in the low to mid 90s are\nforecast, which combined with elevated humidity will likely\nnecessitate additional Heat Advisories for at least our\nsouthern/southeastern counties. Scattered showers and storms are\nalso expected Monday afternoon/evening along the frontal\nboundary. Drier and slightly cooler conditions arrive Tuesday\ninto midweek.",
